<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Susa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Susa was an ancient city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and later an Achaemenid royal capital. It was the eastern terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a> and an important Achaemenid administrative centre. In the seventh century BCE it was sacked by the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, an event linked to Assyrian campaigns against Elam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/susa.jpg" alt="Susa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from Frankfurt, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Apadana,_a_large_hypostyle_room_of_36_columns_in_the_Palace_of_Darius,_Susa,_capital_of_Elam_and_of_the_Achaemenid_Empire,_Iran_(46950284624).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal couriers on the 2,700-kilometre Royal Road from <b>Susa</b> to Sardis could cover the distance in around a week, using relays of fresh riders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…stele about 2.25 metres high, probably first set up at Sippar and later carried off as booty to <b>Susa</b> — was both a practical legal statement and a political manifesto: its prologue declares that Hammurabi was called by the gods “to make justice appear in the land, to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The lowland capital of <b>Susa</b>, heavily influenced by Mesopotamian culture, coexisted with the highland core centred on Anshan, and this bifurcation meant that Elam could lose its lowlands to conquest while retaining an unconquered interior from which revival was always possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lydia became the Achaemenid satrapy of Sparda, with Sardis as the western capital of the Persian Empire and the end of the Royal Road from <b>Susa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Shulgi, the army undertook repeated campaigns in the Zagros against peoples such as the Lullubi and the city of Simurrum, while <b>Susa</b> and parts of Elam were brought under Ur’s control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sarianidi saw it as Indo-Iranian, the result of a migration from southwestern Iran, while Lamberg-Karlovsky saw the BMAC objects at <b>Susa</b>, Shahdad and Tepe Yahya as intrusive.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
